{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02-gcounter.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "The G-Counter",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find a state for a counter whose join keeps every increment exactly once, and see why one slot per replica is forced.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 1 left the like counter broken. Both merges of a single number fail:\n- **max** forgets: two phones that each counted one tap hold 1 and 1, and $\\max(1, 1) = 1$;\n- **sum** double-counts: it is not idempotent, so a state delivered twice adds its taps twice.\n\nMax has the right laws; the state is too poor. Two 1s could be the same tap heard twice or two different taps, and max has to assume the first.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> What could the state record so that the larger of two states never mixes up two different taps? Think about who made each tap.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "One slot per replica",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Record the taps **per replica**. Every replica keeps a slot for every replica and only ever increments its own. Then each slot has a single writer, so of two values for slot $a$ the larger one already counts every tap the smaller one does: max, slot by slot, is exactly right. The value is the sum of the slots. For two replicas a state is a pair, and the states form a product of chains:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let C4 = chain(4)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let G = product(C4, C4)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(G, (2, 0), (1, 3))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Replica $a$ has counted 2 taps and heard of none of $b$'s; replica $b$ has heard of 1 of $a$'s and counted 3 of its own. Merged: $(2, 3)$, value 5, every tap once. Merging an older state such as $(1, 0)$, or the same state again, changes nothing:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(G, (2, 3), (1, 0))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] G-Counter\n> A **G-Counter** (grow-only counter) on replicas $1, \\dots, R$ is a vector of $R$ naturals, all zero at the start. Replica $i$ increments only entry $i$; merge is the entrywise maximum; the value is the sum of the entries.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> Why only its own slot? Let any replica bump any slot. From $(1, 0)$, replicas $a$ and $b$ each count a tap in slot $a$, and both hold $(2, 0)$. Their merge:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(G, (2, 0), (2, 0))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Three taps, value 2: the lost update is back, because slot $a$ now has two writers.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The lost update, fixed",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 1's schedule, on a G-Counter. The work shows each state as a map from replicas to their slots:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "replicas(gcounter; a, b\n  a: inc\n  b: inc\n  a -> b\n  b -> a\n)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Merges need not be symmetric or complete. Step through three replicas: `a` sends to `b`, and `b` to `c`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "replicas(gcounter; a, b, c\n  a: inc\n  b: inc\n  b: inc\n  a -> b\n  b -> c\n)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> `b` and `c` read 3, but `a` has heard from no one and reads 1. Which single message makes all three agree?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "replicas(gcounter; a, b, c\n  a: inc\n  b: inc\n  b: inc\n  a -> b\n  b -> c\n  c -> a\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The chapter opens with the two failed merges as theorems, `max_undercounts` and `add_overcounts`, and then builds the G-Counter as a function from replicas to naturals.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/- ============================================================\n   Crdt.GCounter — Chapter 3: Counting Without Coordination\n   ============================================================ -/",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/- Two refutations first; they derive the design.\n\n   Refuted: \"a distributed counter is a Nat with merge = max\".\n   Both replicas increment once from 0; the merge remembers one event. -/\ntheorem max_undercounts : Nat.max (0 + 1) (0 + 1) ≠ 2 := by decide",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/- Refuted: \"then use merge = (+)\".\n   Addition is not idempotent, so a duplicated delivery double-counts:\n   a replica holding 1 that receives its own state again jumps to 2. -/\ntheorem add_overcounts : 1 + 1 ≠ (1 : Nat) := by decide\n\n/-- The G-Counter: remember one tally *per replica*.\n    An indexed family — your first dependent-indexed carrier. -/\ndef GCounter (R : Nat) : Type := Fin R → Nat\n\nnamespace GCounter\n\nopen Order.PartialOrder Order.BoundedJoinSemilattice\n\nvariable {R : Nat}\n\ninstance : LE (GCounter R) := ⟨fun g h => ∀ i, g i ≤ h i⟩\n\ninstance : Order.PartialOrder (GCounter R) where\n  le_refl g i        := Nat.le_refl (g i)\n  le_antisymm h₁ h₂  := funext fun i => Nat.le_antisymm (h₁ i) (h₂ i)\n  le_trans h₁ h₂ i   := Nat.le_trans (h₁ i) (h₂ i)\n\ninstance : BoundedJoinSemilattice (GCounter R) where\n  sup g h            := fun i => Nat.max (g i) (h i)\n  bot                := fun _ => 0\n  le_sup_left g h i  := Nat.le_max_left (g i) (h i)\n  le_sup_right g h i := Nat.le_max_right (g i) (h i)\n  sup_le _ _ _ h₁ h₂ := fun i => Nat.max_le.mpr ⟨h₁ i, h₂ i⟩\n  bot_le g i         := Nat.zero_le (g i)\n\n/-- Replica `i` increments: bump exactly your own slot. -/\ndef increment (i : Fin R) (g : GCounter R) : GCounter R :=\n  fun j => if j = i then g j + 1 else g j\n\n/-- The query: total of all per-replica tallies. -/\ndef value (g : GCounter R) : Nat :=\n  (List.finRange R).foldl (fun acc i => acc + g i) 0\n\n/-- Render the state for the interludes. -/\ndef toList (g : GCounter R) : List Nat :=\n  (List.finRange R).map g\n\ntheorem increment_inflationary (i : Fin R) :\n    Order.Inflationary (increment i) := by\n  intro g j\n  by_cases h : j = i <;> simp [increment, h]\n\ntheorem increment_monotone {g h : GCounter R} (i : Fin R) (hle : g ⊑ h) :\n    increment i g ⊑ increment i h := by\n  intro j\n  by_cases hj : j = i <;> simp [increment, hj] <;> exact hle _\n\n/-- Merging can only help: an increment survives any merge. -/\ntheorem increment_le_sup (i : Fin R) (g h : GCounter R) :\n    increment i g ⊑ increment i g ⊔ h :=\n  le_sup_left (increment i g) h",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/- ── Fold helpers (generic; reused in Chapters 8, 9, 11) ──── -/\n\ntheorem foldl_add_shift {ι : Type} (f : ι → Nat) :\n    ∀ (l : List ι) (a : Nat),\n      l.foldl (fun acc j => acc + f j) (a + 1)\n        = l.foldl (fun acc j => acc + f j) a + 1\n  | [], _ => rfl\n  | j :: t, a => by\n      rw [List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_cons, Nat.add_right_comm]\n      exact foldl_add_shift f t (a + f j)\n\ntheorem foldl_add_congr {ι : Type} {f g : ι → Nat} :\n    ∀ (l : List ι), (∀ j ∈ l, f j = g j) → ∀ (a : Nat),\n      l.foldl (fun acc j => acc + f j) a\n        = l.foldl (fun acc j => acc + g j) a\n  | [], _, _ => rfl\n  | j :: t, h, a => by\n      rw [List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_cons, h j List.mem_cons_self]\n      exact foldl_add_congr t (fun k hk => h k (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hk)) _\n\ntheorem foldl_add_le {ι : Type} {f g : ι → Nat} (h : ∀ j, f j ≤ g j) :\n    ∀ (l : List ι) {a b : Nat}, a ≤ b →\n      l.foldl (fun acc j => acc + f j) a ≤ l.foldl (fun acc j => acc + g j) b\n  | [], _, _, hab => hab\n  | j :: t, a, b, hab => by\n      rw [List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_cons]\n      exact foldl_add_le h t (Nat.add_le_add hab (h j))\n\n/-- Bump one slot of the summand and the sum grows by exactly one.\n    Proved by induction on `R` via `finRange_succ` — no Nodup needed. -/\ntheorem foldl_add_bump (n : Nat) :\n    ∀ (f g : Fin n → Nat) (i : Fin n),\n      (∀ j, j ≠ i → f j = g j) → f i = g i + 1 → ∀ (a : Nat),\n      (List.finRange n).foldl (fun acc j => acc + f j) a\n        = (List.finRange n).foldl (fun acc j => acc + g j) a + 1 := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => intro _ _ i; exact i.elim0\n  | succ n ih =>\n      intro f g i hne hbump a\n      rw [List.finRange_succ, List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_cons,\n          List.foldl_map, List.foldl_map]\n      cases i using Fin.cases with\n      | zero =>\n          rw [hbump, ← Nat.add_assoc]\n          calc (List.finRange n).foldl (fun acc j => acc + f j.succ) (a + g 0 + 1)\n              = (List.finRange n).foldl (fun acc j => acc + f j.succ) (a + g 0) + 1 :=\n                foldl_add_shift _ _ _\n            _ = (List.finRange n).foldl (fun acc j => acc + g j.succ) (a + g 0) + 1 := by\n                rw [foldl_add_congr (List.finRange n)\n                      (fun j _ => hne j.succ (Fin.succ_ne_zero j))]\n      | succ i' =>\n          rw [hne 0 (Ne.symm (Fin.succ_ne_zero i'))]\n          exact ih (fun (j : Fin n) => f j.succ) (fun (j : Fin n) => g j.succ) i'\n            (fun j hj => hne j.succ (fun hc => hj (Fin.succ_inj.mp hc)))\n            hbump (a + g 0)\n\n/-- The G-Counter counts: every increment raises the value by exactly one,\n    no matter which replica performed it. -/\ntheorem value_increment (i : Fin R) (g : GCounter R) :\n    value (increment i g) = value g + 1 :=\n  foldl_add_bump R (increment i g) g i\n    (fun j hj => by simp [increment, hj])\n    (by simp [increment]) 0\n\n/-- The G-Counter's query happens to be monotone.\n    (Chapter 4 shows this is a coincidence, not a law.) -/\ntheorem value_le_value_of_le {g h : GCounter R} (hle : g ⊑ h) :\n    value g ≤ value h :=\n  foldl_add_le hle (List.finRange R) (Nat.le_refl 0)\n\ntheorem value_bot : value (⊥ : GCounter R) = 0 := by\n  have h : ∀ (l : List (Fin R)) (a : Nat),\n      l.foldl (fun acc i => acc + (⊥ : GCounter R) i) a = a := by\n    intro l\n    induction l with\n    | nil => intro a; rfl\n    | cons j t ih => intro a; rw [List.foldl_cons]; exact ih _\n  exact h _ 0\n\n/-- Interlude: three replicas, out-of-order and duplicated merges. -/\ndef interlude : List (String × List Nat × Nat) :=\n  let gA := increment 0 (increment 0 (⊥ : GCounter 3))  -- A increments twice\n  let gB := increment 1 (⊥ : GCounter 3)                 -- B increments once\n  let gC := increment 2 (⊥ : GCounter 3)                 -- C increments once\n  let atA := (gA ⊔ gB) ⊔ (gC ⊔ gB)   -- B delivered twice\n  let atB := gC ⊔ (gA ⊔ (gB ⊔ gA))   -- another order, A delivered twice\n  let atC := (gB ⊔ gC) ⊔ gA          -- a third order\n  [ (\"replica A\", toList atA, value atA),\n    (\"replica B\", toList atB, value atB),\n    (\"replica C\", toList atC, value atC) ]\n\n#eval interlude\n\nend GCounter",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The executable G-Counter renders as a list; rendering a merge is zipping the renders with $\\max$. The lemma behind it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem zipWith_map_map {α β : Type} (f : β → β → β) (g h : α → β) :\n    ∀ (l : List α), List.zipWith f (l.map g) (l.map h) = l.map (fun i => f (g i) (h i)) := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that zipping two maps of a list is mapping the combined function.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro l\n  induction l with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons x t ih =>\n    simp only [List.map_cons, List.zipWith_cons_cons]\n    rw [ih]",
      "hints": [
        "Induction on the list.",
        "In the `cons` case, `simp only [List.map_cons, List.zipWith_cons_cons]` exposes the head, and the hypothesis handles the tail."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "open Order.PartialOrder Order.BoundedJoinSemilattice in\ntheorem toList_sup {R : Nat} (g h : GCounter R) :\n    GCounter.toList (g ⊔ h)\n      = List.zipWith Nat.max (GCounter.toList g) (GCounter.toList h) :=\n  (zipWith_map_map Nat.max g h (List.finRange R)).symm\n\n/-- A merge's value is at most the sum of the values... -/\ntheorem foldl_add_pair {ι : Type} (f g : ι → Nat) :\n    ∀ (l : List ι) (a b : Nat),\n      l.foldl (fun acc i => acc + (f i + g i)) (a + b)\n        = l.foldl (fun acc i => acc + f i) a\n            + l.foldl (fun acc i => acc + g i) b\n  | [], _, _ => rfl\n  | x :: t, a, b => by\n      rw [List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_cons,\n          show a + b + (f x + g x) = (a + f x) + (b + g x) by omega]\n      exact foldl_add_pair f g t (a + f x) (b + g x)\n\nopen Order.PartialOrder Order.BoundedJoinSemilattice in\ntheorem value_sup_le {R : Nat} (g h : GCounter R) :\n    GCounter.value (g ⊔ h) ≤ GCounter.value g + GCounter.value h := by\n  have hle : ∀ i : Fin R, (g ⊔ h) i ≤ g i + h i := fun i =>\n    Nat.max_le.mpr ⟨Nat.le_add_right _ _, Nat.le_add_left _ _⟩\n  calc GCounter.value (g ⊔ h)\n      ≤ (List.finRange R).foldl (fun acc i => acc + (g i + h i)) 0 :=\n        GCounter.foldl_add_le hle _ (Nat.le_refl 0)\n    _ = GCounter.value g + GCounter.value h := foldl_add_pair g h _ 0 0\n\n/-- ... and strictly less as soon as the two have counted the same taps. -/\nexample :\n    GCounter.value\n        (GCounter.increment 0 (⊥ : GCounter 1) ⊔ GCounter.increment 0 ⊥)\n      < GCounter.value (GCounter.increment 0 (⊥ : GCounter 1))\n          + GCounter.value (GCounter.increment 0 (⊥ : GCounter 1)) := by\n  decide",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(G, (3, 1), (2, 2))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Merge the G-Counter states $(3, 1)$ and $(2, 2)$. Write the pair as `(x, y)`.",
      "hints": [
        "Slot by slot, the maximum."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(G, (1, 3), (1, 2))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Replica $b$ holds $(1, 3)$. A delayed message arrives holding $(1, 2)$, an older state of $b$'s own. Merge it in.",
      "hints": [
        "Slot by slot, the maximum: an older state is below the current one."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A single number cannot be merged: max cannot tell two taps from one tap heard twice, and sum counts repeats. One slot per replica, each with a single writer, makes max right: merge slot by slot, read the sum. Merging twice or merging stale states changes nothing, so the network may duplicate and reorder freely. But every update only adds: how do you count down when every update must go up?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
